On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 02:47:06 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> add acceptable destination addresses: lab@lists.p7x.dev
> freebsd-ports@lists.p7x.dev

Done in patch 9 of "[PATCH v5 0/9] jails/instances/lab-mail: Build the
list's mail hub" [1]. Both addresses join lab-devel@p7x.dev in the
table list in smtpd.conf, so OpenSMTPD delivers them to lists, and
the spec and README.org name them.

Two things follow from it:
- The Fastmail rule that files lab-devel@p7x.dev into lists has to
  cover the two new addresses as well. Otherwise mail sent to them
  from outside stays in Fastmail's INBOX, which mbsync does not sync.
  That rule is in your account, so it is yours to change.
- A message sent to two of the list's addresses lands in lists twice,
  since OpenSMTPD delivers each recipient on its own. notmuch shows it
  once, because both copies share a Message-ID, and the spec now says
  so. If you want one copy, Pigeonhole's duplicate extension can drop
  the second in lists.sieve (four lines, keyed on Message-ID). I left
  it out because I cannot run Dovecot here to test it.

> dovecot is delivering to inbox, but I don't think that mbsync syncs the
> inbox. Though to be fair I don't quite get how dovecot and mbsync work
> together here.

You're right, mbsync does not sync INBOX. Dovecot serves one mail
store, and both posting and syncing go through it:

  client --SMTP--> OpenSMTPD --LMTP--> Dovecot --> INBOX or lists
  Dovecot lists, Archive <--IMAP--> mbsync <--IMAPS--> Fastmail

mbsync is an IMAP client of both Dovecot and Fastmail. Its channel
names only lists and Archive (Patterns lists Archive in mbsyncrc), so
anything in INBOX stays on lab-mail.

> - mbsync syncs messages from fastmail to /data/mail/maildir
> - smtp gets delivered to /data/mail/dovecot/inbox/new

Both are what the config does. /data/mail/maildir is the root of
Dovecot's mail store, so lists lives in /data/mail/maildir/lists, and
mbsync writes there through Dovecot. mail_location in dovecot.conf
keeps INBOX apart, in /data/mail/dovecot/inbox. LMTP on its own
delivers only to INBOX.

> I don't know how that message is supposed to get synced to fastmail.

The Sieve patch is what gets it there: lists.sieve files each post
into lists instead of INBOX, and the next sync carries it to Fastmail.
That patch was last in v3 and v4, as a proposal on top, so a tree
without it has exactly the gap you saw. The v2 and v3 cover letters
named this gap, but the implementation patch did not make the cost
clear.

v5 moves the Sieve patch up to patch 7, right after OpenSMTPD, so the
INBOX-only step is one patch wide and the next patch reads as its fix.
Nothing else changes, as the range-diff in the cover shows. I
recommend taking it. The other way, with no Sieve, is to let mbsync
push INBOX into Fastmail's lists. Then each post would come back on
the next sync and land in lists a second time, unless INBOX were
expunged after each push, so it is more moving parts than four lines
of Sieve.

Any posts already in INBOX can be moved to lists from your login,
pat@p7x.dev, with any IMAP client. The next sync then carries them to
Fastmail.

Thanks for testing it on the jail. Dovecot is not installed here, so
your run is the first real one past the config checks.

